  "faqs": [
    { "q": "What are my obligations?", "a": "If you are responsible for a building or its water systems, you have a legal duty of care to manage the risk from Legionella and to keep people safe, under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974 and the Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations. In practice that means understanding the risk in your systems, controlling it, and being able to show what you have done. We help you meet that duty in a way that is proportionate to your building rather than burdensome." },
    { "q": "Which standards apply to me?", "a": "The central reference is the Approved Code of Practice L8, which sets out how to control the risk from Legionella in water systems. Depending on your building and supply, other frameworks also apply, including BS 8550:2010, BSRIA BG29/2012, the Water Supply (Water Fittings) Regulations 1999 and, for private supplies, the Private Water Supplies Regulations 2009, all underpinned by the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974. We help you identify which of these bear on your site so nothing is overlooked." },
    { "q": "How often does water need testing?", "a": "As a general guide, a Legionella risk assessment is typically reviewed every two years, with an annual review in between to confirm that circumstances have not changed. Sampling and monitoring within that period depend on the system and the level of risk it carries, so the right interval is the one set by your risk assessment rather than a fixed rule. We advise on a schedule that suits your building and keeps you compliant." },
    { "q": "Where does Legionella risk actually arise?", "a": "The risk applies to a range of systems, including cooling towers, pools and spas, and ordinary hot and cold water services. Anywhere water is stored, allowed to stagnate, or held at temperatures that suit bacterial growth deserves attention. Part of our role is to identify which parts of your system carry the greatest risk so that control is focused where it matters." },
    { "q": "What happens if I do nothing?", "a": "Failing to manage the risk is not only dangerous, it is a serious legal exposure. Where negligence leads to harm, the consequences can extend as far as a charge of corporate manslaughter, quite apart from the human cost of an outbreak. Managing the risk properly, and being able to show it, is far less costly than the alternative." },
    { "q": "Who is responsible, me or my contractor?", "a": "The legal duty rests with the person in control of the premises or the water system, and that responsibility cannot be handed over simply by appointing a contractor. What a good contractor does is help you discharge that duty competently, with the right assessments, controls and records in place. We work alongside you so the obligations are met, while making clear where the responsibility sits." },
